<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Assets' cross-module contracts, and NOTHING else.

    Named .PublicApi and not .Contracts because there is already a business
    module called Contracts - maintenance contracts and AMC - and a suffix
    that cannot tell a module from a contract assembly is a rule no test can
    enforce. .PublicApi is also the word doc 01 rule 3 already uses.

    Rule 2 forbids a module referencing another module, and rule 3 says to
    depend on its PublicApi contract instead. Those two are only compatible if
    the contract lives somewhere a module may reference - which is here.

    What belongs: the interface another module calls, and the DTOs it passes.
    What does not: EF Core, DbContext, entities, or any implementation. The
    architecture tests check that, because a contract assembly that grows an EF
    dependency drags it into every consumer and quietly rebuilds the coupling
    this project exists to remove.

    One assembly per PUBLISHING module, not one shared AMS.PublicApi. With
    fifteen modules a single shared project would be referenced by all of them,
    and "which modules does this one actually depend on" would stop being
    answerable from the build - the exact question schema-per-module exists to
    keep answerable. See docs/00DESIGNDECISIONS.md.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\AMS.SharedKernel\AMS.SharedKernel.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
